(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-4-heart-failure-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Heart Failure Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 4","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 4","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"After acute heart failure from infarction leaves the ejection fraction below 35%, what comes first for sudden death protection?","choices":["Home inotropes until the heart recovers","A wearable defibrillator while therapy is optimized","An implantable defibrillator before discharge","Heart transplant listing at the first admission"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The pathway is a wearable defibrillator vest, then optimized guideline-directed therapy and cardiac rehabilitation, then a repeat echocardiogram, and an implantable defibrillator only if the ejection fraction has not improved. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"After diuretics, which drug class has the next strongest recommendation in preserved ejection fraction?","choices":["Hydralazine with a nitrate","Sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor","Mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist","Angiotensin II receptor blocker"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In preserved ejection fraction, diuretics as needed are strong and the sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor is moderate. The neprilysin combination, the mineralocorticoid antagonist and the receptor blocker are weak. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which ejection fraction range defines heart failure with reduced ejection fraction?","choices":["41% to 49%","50% or more","55% to 70%","40% or less"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Reduced ejection fraction is 40% or less, mildly reduced is 41 to 49%, and preserved is 50% or more. The reduced group carries the fullest set of strongly recommended drug classes. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which of these is a risk factor for heart failure?","choices":["Regular aerobic exercise","Low dietary potassium","Chronic bradycardia","Chronic tachyarrhythmias"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Chronic tachyarrhythmias sit on the risk factor list with age, hypertension, coronary artery disease, diabetes, obesity, alcohol, tobacco, family history, genetics and cardiotoxic medicines. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"For a patient at risk for heart failure who has diabetes, which drug class is recommended first?","choices":["Sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor","Beta blocker as the first agent","Mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist","Loop diuretic such as furosemide"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In stage A, at risk for heart failure, the one strongly recommended drug is a sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor in patients with diabetes. A beta blocker enters only at stage B, once structural disease exists. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A patient with long-standing heart failure develops a sudden worsening. How is the episode classified?","choices":["Acute on chronic heart failure","Acute new-onset heart failure","Pre-heart failure with new symptoms","High-output heart failure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In the sequence pre-heart failure, acute new onset, resolution, exacerbation, chronic heart failure and acute on chronic heart failure, a sudden worsening on a background of chronic disease is acute on chronic heart failure. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which drug class carries a strong recommendation in stage B but not in stage A?","choices":["Hydralazine-nitrate","Beta blocker","Sodium-glucose inhibitor","Loop diuretic"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Stage B, pre-heart failure, adds an angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor and a beta blocker, with an angiotensin receptor blocker if the inhibitor is not tolerated. Stage A carries only the sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor for diabetes. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"What defines New York Heart Association class I?","choices":["No symptoms","Symptoms with exertion","Symptoms with slight exertion","Symptoms at rest"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Class I is no symptoms, class II is symptoms with exertion, class III is symptoms with slight exertion, and class IV is symptoms at rest. The classes grade symptoms, not structure. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What proportion of heart failure is left-sided?","choices":["About half","Nearly 100%","70 to 80%","10 to 20%"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Left-sided heart failure is by far the most common, at 70 to 80% of cases, and it is also the most common cause of right-sided heart failure. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which heart failure stage is also called pre-heart failure?","choices":["Stage A","Stage C","Stage D","Stage B"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Stage B, pre-heart failure, is structural heart disease without symptoms, such as ischemic cardiomyopathy or left ventricular hypertrophy. Its therapy adds renin-angiotensin blockade and a beta blocker. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"What defines high-output heart failure?","choices":["Normal output with high filling pressures","Low output from a stiff, thick ventricle","Elevated output from faster rate and contraction","Low output from weak ventricular contraction"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. High-output failure is elevated cardiac output from increased heart rate and contractility, meeting an increased peripheral demand; over time it may lead to ischemic cardiomyopathy or remodeling. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which natriuretic peptide result is altered by an angiotensin receptor-neprilysin inhibitor?","choices":["B-type natriuretic peptide","N-terminal pro-B-type natriuretic peptide","Both peptides to the same degree","Neither peptide in any meaningful way"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Neprilysin degrades B-type natriuretic peptide, so blocking it with sacubitril-valsartan alters that result. Knowing whether the test was the peptide or its N-terminal fragment matters in these patients. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"How is congestive heart failure diagnosed?","choices":["By a natriuretic peptide level interpreted on its own","Clinically, from signs and symptoms plus supporting evidence","By an ejection fraction measurement on echocardiography alone","By right heart catheterization performed in every patient"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Congestive heart failure is a clinical diagnosis: signs and symptoms of heart failure plus an elevated B-type natriuretic peptide or cardiogenic pulmonary or systemic congestion. No single test makes the diagnosis alone. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Which heart failure risk factors can a patient change?","choices":["Age, genetics and prior infarction","Family history and advancing age","Tobacco, excess alcohol and obesity","Age, family history and genetics"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. These are modifiable, so they are the targets of prevention counseling. Age, family history and genetic abnormality are risk factors a patient cannot change. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Why is a complete metabolic panel ordered in heart failure?","choices":["To exclude myocardial infarction","To follow potassium and renal function","To measure the natriuretic peptide","To detect anemia as a contributor"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The panel tracks electrolytes, potassium above all, and renal function through blood urea nitrogen, creatinine and filtration rate. Both matter because diuretics and several therapies move potassium and affect the kidneys. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What is the lifetime risk of developing heart failure?","choices":["About one in four","About one in twenty","About one in ten","About one in two"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Lifetime risk is 24%, about one in four, and about 6.7 million Americans older than 20 have heart failure. The burden rises steeply with age. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which findings reflect portal overload in right-sided heart failure?","choices":["Peripheral edema and distended neck veins","Orthopnea, crackles and a third heart sound","Narrow pulse pressure and cool extremities","Nausea, poor appetite and hepatomegaly"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Right ventricular failure backs fluid up. Portal overload gives nausea, loss of appetite and hepatomegaly with congestion or ascites, while systemic overload gives peripheral edema and jugular venous distension. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What does cardiac remodeling consist of?","choices":["Myocyte regeneration and scar resorption","Valve calcification and annular widening","Coronary plaque rupture and thrombosis","Myocyte hypertrophy and collagen fibrosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Acute damage or chronic conditions drive remodeling, which is myocyte hypertrophy and collagen fibrosis. Neurohormonal activation, inflammation and mechanical stress push it along. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which drug is the angiotensin receptor-neprilysin inhibitor?","choices":["Hydralazine-dinitrate","Sacubitril-valsartan","Lisinopril alone","Valsartan alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Sacubitril-valsartan pairs an angiotensin II receptor blocker with a neprilysin inhibitor. It is recommended in reduced ejection fraction for class II or III, and it alters the B-type natriuretic peptide result. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Which combination reflects core therapy for heart failure with reduced ejection fraction?","choices":["Inotrope, nitrate, diuretic and calcium blocker","Beta blocker, lisinopril, spironolactone, dapagliflozin","Calcium blocker, beta blocker, diuretic and inotrope","Diuretic alone, adding others only for comorbidities"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The core is a beta blocker, a renin-angiotensin blocker such as lisinopril (or an angiotensin receptor-neprilysin inhibitor, converting enzyme inhibitor or receptor blocker), a mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist such as spironolactone and a sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor such as dapagliflozin, with diuretics as needed. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"What is the most common valvular cause of heart failure?","choices":["Tricuspid regurgitation","Pulmonic stenosis","Aortic stenosis","Mitral stenosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Valvular disease is among the causes of heart failure, and aortic stenosis is the most common valvular one. Mitral stenosis appears instead as a cause of right-sided failure. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which N-terminal pro-B-type natriuretic peptide level suggests heart failure in a patient older than 75?","choices":["Above 3,000","Above 450","Above 125","Above 100"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Older than 75, heart failure is likely above 450, compared with above 125 in younger patients. The higher threshold is the one age-specific rule for elderly patients. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"How is high-output heart failure treated?","choices":["Treat the underlying cause and add a diuretic","Inotropes in the intensive care unit","A calcium channel blocker to slow the rate","Early referral for heart transplant"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. High-output failure is driven by peripheral demand, so treatment targets the cause, such as anemia, hyperthyroidism or an arteriovenous fistula, with a diuretic for congestion. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Why is a beta blocker the wrong first choice for an at-risk patient with diabetes and a normal heart?","choices":["Diabetes makes any drug unnecessary","A loop diuretic must be started first","The first drug is a sodium-glucose inhibitor","Beta blockers are banned in all heart failure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In stage A the only strongly recommended drug is a sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor in patients with diabetes. The beta blocker is a stage B therapy once structural disease exists. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"How does the murmur of tricuspid regurgitation change with breathing?","choices":["Louder on expiration","Silent on inspiration","Unchanged by breathing","Louder on inspiration"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Tricuspid regurgitation, which accompanies pulmonary arterial hypertension in right-sided failure, is a holosystolic murmur at the left lower sternal border that grows louder on inspiration. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What does a B-type natriuretic peptide level below 100 indicate?","choices":["Acute heart failure is ruled out","Heart failure is likely","Acute heart failure is likely","The result is indeterminate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A level below 100 rules out acute heart failure with a negative predictive value above 90%. From 100 to 400 the test has no useful sensitivity or specificity, above 400 heart failure is likely, and above 900 acute heart failure is likely. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which symptom describes waking from sleep short of breath?","choices":["Paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea","Orthopnea","Dyspnea on exertion","Exertional near-syncope"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea is waking at night breathless. Orthopnea is breathlessness lying flat, and dyspnea on exertion comes with effort; all three are the dyspnea of heart failure. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which New York Heart Association class describes symptoms at rest?","choices":["Class III","Class I","Class IV","Class II"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Class IV is symptoms at rest, the most limited functional class. Class III is symptoms with slight exertion and class II is symptoms with exertion. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What is systolic heart failure?","choices":["Failure of the valves to close","Failure of the atria to contract","Failure of contraction, the squeeze","Failure of relaxation, the rest"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Systole is the squeeze, so systolic heart failure is dysfunction of contraction. Diastole is the rest, so diastolic failure is dysfunction of relaxation. That one distinction drives the rest of the chain. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"How is guideline-directed therapy adjusted in reduced ejection fraction?","choices":["Started one class per year only","Held until symptoms fully resolve","Given at maximum from day one","Optimized as hemodynamically tolerated"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Therapy in reduced ejection fraction is prioritized for the patient's comorbidities and optimized as hemodynamically stable, meaning each class is increased as blood pressure and tolerance allow. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 56"}]};
